---
// What else it does, as the instrument's spec sheet: grouped definition lists, no cards. Facts come from
// the README's "What it does" and "Privacy" sections, checked against the 1.0 source: the default keys
// (ink-ffi's voice.rs), detection's offer and "Record now" (the Mac's MeetingModel), the retention
// choices (retention.days) and the consents (ink_pipeline::consent).
---

<section class="features wrap" id="features" aria-labelledby="features-title">
  <div class="section-head">
    <h2 id="features-title">What else it does.</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      Two jobs, dictation and meeting notes, both on your machine. The features that need a language model run
      on Apple’s on-device model on the Mac, and each asks for your OK first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="spec-group">
    <h3>Dictation</h3>
    <dl class="spec">
      <div>
        <dt>Anywhere you type</dt>
        <dd>
          Hold a key, speak, let go: the text is typed into the focused app. The key is a modifier held on its
          own, <kbd>Fn</kbd> on the Mac and right <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> on Windows until you pick another.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The Drop</dt>
        <dd>A small window that shows your words as you say them, and never takes the focus from the app you are typing in.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Modes</dt>
        <dd>How the text is written can follow the app you are typing into.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Snippets</dt>
        <dd>Trigger phrases that expand to full text.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="spec-wide">
        <dt>Polish and voice edit, on the Mac</dt>
        <dd>
          Optional and off by default. Polish tidies a dictation before it is typed. Voice edit, on a key of its
          own, rewrites the text you selected to a spoken instruction. Both run on Apple’s on-device model, so
          your words stay on the Mac.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <div class="spec-group">
    <h3>Meetings</h3>
    <dl class="spec">
      <div>
        <dt>Offered, never started for you</dt>
        <dd>
          When an app has held the microphone for a few seconds, the Drop offers to record. Nothing records
          until you say so, and “Record now” starts one by hand.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Two streams</dt>
        <dd>Your microphone is you and the call’s audio is them, so who said what is never a guess on your side.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Live, then final</dt>
        <dd>
          A live transcript while the call runs, with notes you can add. When it ends, a final pass replaces the
          live words and labels the other side’s speakers when there are two or more.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Summary, Owed and Ask, on the Mac</dt>
        <dd>
          With your OK, a summary with the decisions and actions it cites, the promises made in the call kept
          under Owed, and answers to your questions about the meeting.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <div class="spec-group">
    <h3>What it keeps</h3>
    <dl class="spec">
      <div>
        <dt>The library</dt>
        <dd>
          Every dictation and meeting, newest first and searchable. A meeting’s record shows its transcript,
          notes and summary, and plays its audio from disk.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>For as long as you choose</dt>
        <dd>Keep records forever, the default, or for 7, 30, 90 or 365 days.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <div class="spec-group">
    <h3>What it does not do</h3>
    <ul class="not-built">
      <li>Run on Linux or on an Intel Mac. Inkwell 0.2 stays the last version for them.</li>
      <li>Send your words to a cloud model. The language-model features run on the Mac’s own.</li>
      <li>Transcribe an audio or video file, or export to TXT, SRT, JSON or CSV, as 0.2 did.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
